  {
   "n": 4,
   "type": "MCQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33",
   "html": "<p>Which of the following static V-I characteristics represents a Bilateral device?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/29/29cc4d4988c8d488fa45.png\" />"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c2aca96f10b889831c3c.png\" />"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/9e/9e7005d5be04a8cb3300.png\" />"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/2349f78a97dd58b9ed42.png\" />"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "D"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "D",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0.33
   },
   "solution": "<p>(d)<br />\nWhen the same relation exists between voltage and current for the current flowing in both directions or in other words, if the VI characteristics curve is similar in opposite quadrants then the element is bidirectional (or) bilateral. Hence, option (d) is correct.</p>"
  },
  {
   "n": 5,
   "type": "MCQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33",
   "html": "<p>To analyze a complex network which consists of a supernode, which of the following laws are required to write the supernodal equations?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "KVL and Ohm’s law"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "KCL and Ohm’s law"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "KVL, KCL and Ohm’s law"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "KVL and KCL"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "C",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0.33
   },
   "solution": "<p>(c)<br />\nWhen only an ideal voltage source is connected between two non-reference nodes without any impedance, we form a supernode encompassing these two nodes for applying KCL while the voltage between the nodes are related by KVL. Thus, supernodal equations require applications of KCL, KVL and Ohm&rsquo;s law.</p>"
  },
